WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.010
"What did you do to me? Your body won't last long. You'll be stuck in there for the rest of your life"

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:10.558
"[cat] You get me out of here you son of a- [man] Calm down Mr. Brand. You're a cat. [cat] I hate cats"

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:18.640
I hate to waste my money or my time on something that is truly bad like

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:23.660
Suicide Squad bad, where every decision seems to be made for maximum shift

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:27.220
"Somethin' tells me a whole lot *beep* people are about to die."

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:32.540
It's a shame, but in the end, I think crappy movies are an inevitable part of the cinematic ecosystem.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.440
You're not ever gonna get away from them.

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:37.340
And whether or not they make money, some of which unfortunately do,

00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:41.180
No one is setting out to make a movie that is a piece of crap.

00:00:41.180 --> 00:00:44.020
"I'm talking about a completely superfluous bottle of cough syrup...

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:46.920
That's like six bucks"

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:50.460
"Are you joking?"

00:00:50.469 --> 00:00:54.240
What's more insidious in my opinion are movies that are just... Passable.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.760
Films just good enough not to be terrible.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:00.840
I mean passable films, they aren't bad they might even be better than okay

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:06.300
But they're a far cry from great, or noteworthy, or something that you would like to see more than once.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:09.120
They're the cinematic equivalent of this image

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.360
They don't fall apart, but their balance is always precarious

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:18.040
In the last handful of years, my annoyance with passable movies has steadily increased in proportion

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.700
with their steadily increasing number

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.820
Passable films like to multiply.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.140
They do just enough for the audience, and just enough for the studio, and just enough for the stars

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.040
They have no problem being funded.

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:32.840
[Kevin Hart] "Stop Stop! I need to know what is happening. Right now."

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:39.120
For a long time, I've attributed the major problem with passable movies  to a lack of tonal control.

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.060
the emotional logic of the narrative in films like these often, often feels a bit out of whack

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.680
Either the film tries to be too many things at once leading to unintended emotional confusion

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.060
"John Francis Walker, by authority of Governor Nicks...

00:01:52.060 --> 00:01:53.780
this unit has been author..." [girl grunts] *CLANG* "ized..."

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:54.820
...to extinguish your life

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.600
*CLANG*CLANG*CLANG*

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.560
*CLANG**ZAPZAP*CLANG*ZAP*CLANG*

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.900
*zap*clang*clang*clang*clang*

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.900
*CLANG*GNALC*ZAP*ZAP*CLANG*CLANG

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.360
[man quietly] "oh..okay...okay..."

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.040
*CLANG*CLANG*

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:08.420
*still clanging &amp; zapping*...

00:02:08.420 --> 00:02:10.320
[Man]  "alright...thats enough. That's enough."

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:15.500
Or the filmmakers give characters traits that don't belong to them in order to serve some plot function

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:16.900
[Driver] "What has it been this year so far?...

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:17.950
...Bernie Madoff?...

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:23.020
...Two wars without end? Million people with no work? And that's just the first two weeks of January."

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:27.100
Or emotional arcs, initiated early in the story, are abandoned without comment

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.820
often proving in the end to be entirely pointless

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.720
In good movies, everything points to everything else.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:40.220
 

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:47.340
The emotional arcs of the characters, the scenes, the imagery, the music and the plot are tightly entwined

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.760
And this doesn't just go for highbrow movies, by the way.

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:54.360
Whenever you hear someone say of a lower brow film that "it knows what it is"

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.720
What they're really saying is that the film was tonally focused.

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.019
On that, we agree

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:06.220
But recently, I've begun to notice another unifying feature of passable movies

00:03:06.220 --> 00:03:08.480
Indeed, of all passable storytelling.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.517
And to understand what I mean take a look at this scene:

00:03:11.517 --> 00:03:15.260
"I gotta say it feels really good to have somebody looking out for me."

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:16.840
"I've got your back man"

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:17.640
"I like that...

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:20.380
...And I got your back, too"

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:27.460
"Hey Doug...

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:30.680
...I like you man. I think you're a great guy.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:32.780
I just want you to remember that this is a business relationship...

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.320
Doesn't mean that. We're gonna be best friends. It's not how the story ends.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.480
I'm just an employee with a job to do."

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:41.980
[Doug] "I get it.  You're not my best friend. You're just my best man."

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.140
"It doesn't mean we're not gonna have a good time, Doug!"

00:03:45.220 --> 00:03:46.500
[Doug] "I said I got it!

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.140
"You're anybody's best friend for a price, but nobody's when it counts."

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:58.160
[Voice Over] All the beats in this short scene should be familiar to us we've seen them all before

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.500
But there's something odd going on here.

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:05.400
You see, stories are almost always about people

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:08.560
or aliens or gods or animals or robots that act like people

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:13.520
And great stories are always the ones that observe people truthfully

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:17.340
The ones that capture human experience with nuance and insight

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:20.220
And the best stories make such acute observations about humanity

00:04:20.220 --> 00:04:21.840
that they can show us things about ourselves

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.600
that we didn't know or teach us how to articulate those things

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.220
Against a vast. Unintelligible. Anxiety

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:35.500
And this, I think, is the big difference between great movies and passable ones

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:41.180
When passable movies observe human experience, they observe it not through the lens of real life

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:44.540
But through the lens of other movies

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:45.720
"Love is my life."

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:47.380
"No! Love is your job!"

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:51.710
"You know, I think you're threatened by what Zoey and I have...

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:53.420
...because you're afraid I won't be able to hang out every night"

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.360
"You are my drug dealer...

00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:00.530
...There's one reason we know each other. I like the drugs you sell. That's it"

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:06.200
There is this huge library. This huge vocabulary of actions built up over the years

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:07.820
That people you know

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:11.800
Don't really do but which happened so often in TV and movies

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.720
That they're familiar enough to an audience that they become, well...

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:17.540
passable for human motivations

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.749
I'm not saying it's bad for filmmakers to cite or pay homage to the movies that they love in their own work

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:31.060
I'm saying that too many films released today are being cobbled together from a weird alternate reality.

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.460
That is only a dim echo of our own.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.880
I do not believe in Fairy tales about Chakras or energy

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:41.700
Or the power belief there is no such thing as spirit

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:45.120
This is the long-term danger of cliche.

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:48.300
The more we allow it to happen, the harder it is to escape

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:52.640
The more difficult it is for audiences and writers to tell the difference

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:58.980
The last thing we want is to see our own lives through the lens of art that's only passable

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:01.120
New perspectives are what we need.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:03.880
Originality is what we should encourage at every chance.

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:11.080
Otherwise, I'm afraid our movies and ourselves will change into something quite different

